      "provenance": "literature_retrieved",
      "rationale": "[Literature audit 2026-11-05 (deliverome-analysis PR 236), verdict confirmed_exact: position confirmed at this residue. validation_level surface_only -> detected_only (paper reports surface: no, function: not_reported). Paper (PMID 28904854): \"we cloned the coding sequence for the FLAG tag (AAs DYKDDDDK) or the MYC tag (AAs EQKLISEEDL) behind the coding sequence for the first N-terminal 29 AAs of CKAMP44 (including the signal peptide)\". Audit note: Constructs are explicitly mouse Shisa9 (UniProt Q9CZN4, 424 aa); mouse and human residues 1-35 align with zero offset (H29|G30 in both), so the human junction is 29|30. Surface NOT shown: neurons were fixed in PFA and permeabilised with 0.2% Triton before FLAG staining, so validation_level should be detected_only, not surface_only. No function assay.] L1 explicitly states the tag is inserted after the first 29 aa of CKAMP44 including the signal peptide (SP = residues 1\u201323 by topology landmarks), placing the junction after residue 29 (H) and before residue 30 (G), both confirmed by reading the numbered sequence. Python computation confirms residue 29 is extracellular (EC span 24\u2013151), 123 residues from the TM boundary, well clear of all constraints. L3 from the same paper validates surface display by immunofluorescence in neurons. Full-length CKAMP44/Shisa9 constructs in pCMV vector with FLAG (DYKDDDDK) or MYC (EQKLISEEDL) tag inserted after the first 29 aa of CKAMP44 (including the 23-aa signal peptide), placing the tag 6 residues into the extracellular domain of the membrane-anchored protein. FLAG-CKAMP44-FL was expressed in primary hippocampal neurons (DIV 21) and detected by immunofluorescence co-stained with MAP2.",
